Found by the font session while investigating opf-render#118. In WebKit, an RTL title (Arabic or Hebrew) in the SVG preview paints its first letter to the right of its last, which is the wrong visual order for a run the renderer has already laid out. Chromium and the PNG/PDF outputs are correct.

To do:
- reproduce with Playwright webkit on an `ar-SA` and a `he-IL` title;
- check the `direction`/`unicode-bidi` attributes and `textLength` on the `<text>`;
- fix it, or, for WebKit, draw the run with `text: "paths"`. Add a WebKit browser test.

Found by the font session while investigating opf-render#118 (WebKit doesn't kern SVG text). In WebKit, `Range.getBoundingClientRect()` for a character inside the preview's SVG `<text>` returns a rect about 27 px from where the glyph is painted. The editor positions its caret and selection from those rects, so in Safari the caret or selection may land in the wrong place.

To do:
- reproduce in Safari/WebKit (Playwright webkit) on an editable text block;
- confirm whether the editor's caret placement is affected;
- if it is, compute the caret from `getExtentOfChar`/`getStartPositionOfChar` or from the renderer's layout boxes instead of DOM ranges in WebKit.
